      en: "Polis Chrysochous (Greek: Πόλις Χρυσοχούς) is a town in the Paphos District of Cyprus, located on the Chrysochous Bay. Historically, it was the ancient city-kingdom of Marion, founded in the 6th century BC. Today, it is a popular tourist destination known for its beaches, archaeological sites, and the nearby Akamas Peninsula. The town's population is about 4,000 (2011). The economy is driven by tourism, agriculture (especially citrus), and fishing. The Polis Archaeological Museum houses artifacts from the Marion period. The town also hosts an annual wine festival.",
